<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Arabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1165">1165</a>–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Muhyiddin Ibn Arabi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Arabi was an Andalusian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic, philosopher and poet, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1165, who became one of the most influential thinkers in Islamic history. After years of travel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> he settled in the east and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1240. His works, including The Meccan Revelations and The Bezels of Wisdom, developed the doctrine later called the unity of being, and he became known as 'the greatest master'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi.jpg" alt="Ibn Arabi" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar.Hafez · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%8A%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
